WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.512 --> 00:00:08.463
<v Maya>You know, it's kind of this universal truth of modern life that software is just everywhere. And well, software is fundamentally broken.</v>

2
00:00:08.904 --> 00:00:09.545
<v Daniel>Yeah, pretty much.</v>

3
00:00:09.945 --> 00:00:20.844
<v Maya>Like you open an app on your phone and it inexplicably crashes or you load a web page and it just hangs there forever. We all just sort of accept this ambient level of buggy reality.</v>

4
00:00:21.244 --> 00:00:22.705
<v Daniel>It's just part of the deal now, right? Right.</v>

5
00:00:23.326 --> 00:00:36.318
<v Maya>But imagine for a second if you could mathematically prove, like beyond a shadow of a doubt, that a bug simply doesn't exist. Not just that you haven't found it yet through testing, but that it is mathematically impossible for that bug to be there.</v>

6
00:00:36.639 --> 00:00:47.584
<v Daniel>I mean, that would be a complete paradigm shift for the entire technology industry. We are incredibly used to testing software, which is ultimately just a process of looking for the presence of bugs.</v>

7
00:00:47.825 --> 00:00:49.565
<v Maya>Yeah, poking around to see what breaks.</v>

8
00:00:49.826 --> 00:00:54.868
<v Daniel>Exactly. But proving the absence of bugs, that is a completely different universe of certainty.</v>

9
00:00:55.123 --> 00:01:06.025
<v Maya>And that universe of certainty is exactly what we are exploring in our deep dive today. We are unpacking a brand new lab release that was published just yesterday, October 22, 2026.</v>

10
00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:07.185
<v Daniel>Fresh off the presses.</v>

11
00:01:07.405 --> 00:01:21.008
<v Maya>Super fresh. It comes from Anthropic's Frontier Red team, working in collaboration with a startup called Theorem. And to be clear right up front, I want to say this plainly once. This is a lab release, not a peer-reviewed paper.</v>

12
00:01:21.288 --> 00:01:22.288
<v Daniel>Right. Important distinction.</v>

13
00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:37.453
<v Maya>Yeah. The lead authors on this work are Keane Lucas, Nikolai Zeldovich, Nicholas Carlini, and Jason Gross. And they're basically claiming this massive leap in using large language models to formally verify software at</v>

14
00:01:37.473 --> 00:01:37.754
<v Maya>scale.</v>

15
00:01:37.974 --> 00:01:38.594
<v Daniel>Which is huge.</v>

16
00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:52.477
<v Maya>It is. But before we get into the wild AI wizardry of how they actually did this, we really need to set the stage on why. Like, why is the tech world suddenly so desperate for mathematical proofs of safety right now?</v>

17
00:01:52.917 --> 00:02:04.267
<v Daniel>So to understand the urgency, you really have to look back at what happened earlier this year. Back in April 2026, there was this cybersecurity assessment released for the Claude mythos preview model.</v>

18
00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:05.388
<v Maya>I remember that. Yeah.</v>

19
00:02:05.628 --> 00:02:17.418
<v Daniel>Yeah. And the findings from that assessment were genuinely sobering for anyone working in cybersecurity. Large language models have just become terrifyingly adept at finding and exploiting vulnerabilities in code.</v>

20
00:02:17.842 --> 00:02:22.545
<v Maya>And we aren't just talking about, you know, finding simple typos in someone's homework assignment, right?</v>

21
00:02:22.825 --> 00:02:37.334
<v Daniel>Oh, not at all. No. These models are uncovering ancient, like deeply buried architectural flaws in foundational software. During that mythos preview testing, the AI found a 27-year-old bug in</v>

22
00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:38.695
<v Daniel>OpenBSD's TCP</v>

23
00:02:38.735 --> 00:02:40.756
<v Maya>implementation. 27 years old?</v>

24
00:02:40.856 --> 00:02:55.258
<v Daniel>27 years. And OpenBSD is an operating system that's literally famous for its obsessive focus on security. Right. But the AI uncovered this flaw where just a single manipulated packet of data sent over a network could crash the entire machine.</v>

25
00:02:55.578 --> 00:02:55.778
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

26
00:02:56.159 --> 00:03:10.470
<v Daniel>Yeah. So when attackers have access to that kind of automated, relentless, AI-driven bug hunting capability, I mean, human defenders can no longer rely on standard testing methodologies. Finding 99% of the bugs just isn't good enough anymore.</v>

27
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.632
<v Maya>Because the AI is going to find that remaining 1%.</v>

28
00:03:13.367 --> 00:03:23.114
<v Daniel>Exactly. Defenders need ironclad guarantees that the code holding up your online banking or your home Wi-Fi router is actually secure.</v>

29
00:03:23.495 --> 00:03:35.143
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this because the difference between traditional testing and formal proving is, well, it's crucial to this whole release. The standard defense right now across the industry is something called fuzz testing, right?</v>

30
00:03:35.223 --> 00:03:35.804
<v Daniel>Yes, fuzzing.</v>

31
00:03:36.284 --> 00:03:42.469
<v Maya>And to me, fuzz testing is like throwing a million darts at a complicated maze while you're blindfolded just to see where the walls are.</v>

32
00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.771
<v Daniel>That's a pretty good analogy, actually.</v>

33
00:03:45.031 --> 00:03:58.821
<v Maya>Right. Like you might find a whole lot of dead ends, but you never really know if you've mapped the entire thing. But this new formal verification approach they're using, it's like having the architectural blueprint in your hands, mathematically proving that no dead ends exist.</v>

34
00:03:59.181 --> 00:03:59.721
<v Daniel>Precisely.</v>

35
00:04:00.262 --> 00:04:07.467
<v Maya>Can you explain exactly why a proof over all possible inputs is just fundamentally a different kind of evidence than fuzzing?</v>

36
00:04:07.833 --> 00:04:21.178
<v Daniel>Yeah, so it ultimately comes down to the mathematical limits of sampling. A fuzzer operates by generating millions of random or maybe slightly mutated inputs. And it feeds those inputs into a program over and over just to see if any of them cause a crash.</v>

37
00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:22.459
<v Maya>Just throwing darts.</v>

38
00:04:22.799 --> 00:04:37.005
<v Daniel>Right. But a complex program might have an essentially infinite number of possible inputs. Let's say a specific software bug only triggers if you feed it a highly specific 64 megabyte file with, I don't know, one particular byte flipped at the very end.</v>

39
00:04:37.252 --> 00:04:37.592
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

40
00:04:38.193 --> 00:04:51.503
<v Daniel>A fuzzer could run continuously for a decade and never randomly generate that exact file. It is structurally blind to highly specific edge cases. But a formal proof, on the other hand, doesn't actually run the code at all.</v>

41
00:04:51.703 --> 00:04:52.764
<v Maya>Wait, it doesn't run the code?</v>

42
00:04:52.924 --> 00:05:07.713
<v Daniel>No, not at all. It uses mathematical logic to analyze the fundamental structure of the program itself. So it evaluates the logic and states, definitively, for every possible input within these defined parameters, a crash cannot logically</v>

43
00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:07.993
<v Daniel>occur.</v>

44
00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:13.554
<v Maya>So it covers the entire landscape of possibilities all at once rather than just sampling random spots.</v>

45
00:05:13.734 --> 00:05:27.657
<v Daniel>Yes. That total coverage is exactly what Claude achieved here. What the lab release details is how Claude took existing fuzz tests from Google's OSS Fuzz Initiative and instead of just running them, turned them into formal specifications.</v>

46
00:05:28.515 --> 00:05:29.776
<v Maya>Okay, what does that mean in practice?</v>

47
00:05:30.156 --> 00:05:43.844
<v Daniel>Basically, they are mathematical statements saying this specific test can never crash for any input under 64 megabytes. And then Claude wrote machine-checked mathematical proofs in this specialized language called ROC.</v>

48
00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:46.385
<v Maya>Right, ROC, R-O-C-Q.</v>

49
00:05:46.666 --> 00:05:50.308
<v Daniel>Yeah, and it did this for 239 different entry points across 123 open-source software libraries.</v>

50
00:05:54.705 --> 00:06:08.195
<v Maya>And here's where it gets really interesting to me. Because they didn't do this on the C or C++ source code that human developers actually write and read. Claude wrote these proofs at the level of compiled RISC-V binaries.</v>

51
00:06:08.255 --> 00:06:09.396
<v Daniel>Yes, the binary level.</v>

52
00:06:09.876 --> 00:06:22.105
<v Maya>Wait, but reading compiled machine code, like the raw ones and zeros, that seems so much harder than reading the source code. It's just an endless sea of granular instructions. Why do this at the binary level?</v>

53
00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:37.568
<v Daniel>because the binary is what actually runs on the physical microchip. This is a classic persistent problem in software verification. How so? Well, think of the C++ source code as a recipe, and the compiled binary as the actual</v>

54
00:06:37.689 --> 00:06:47.591
<v Daniel>cake that comes out of the oven. A program called a compiler is what translates the recipe into the cake. But compilers themselves are software. And</v>

55
00:06:47.851 --> 00:06:48.772
<v Maya>compilers have bugs.</v>

56
00:06:49.172 --> 00:07:04.020
<v Daniel>Exactly. Compilers have bugs. They also optimize things in weird, unpredictable ways to make the code run faster. So if you only prove that the source code, the recipe, is safe, you're assuming the compiler perfectly translated your intentions.</v>

57
00:07:05.188 --> 00:07:08.611
<v Maya>Ah, so you still have a massive translation risk if you only look at the source code.</v>

58
00:07:08.771 --> 00:07:16.798
<v Daniel>You do. By operating directly at the binary level, looking at the exact RISC-V machine instructions, they completely remove that risk of translation error.</v>

59
00:07:17.118 --> 00:07:17.959
<v Maya>That makes perfect sense.</v>

60
00:07:18.459 --> 00:07:27.307
<v Daniel>Yeah. The proof applies to the actual ones and zeros that the CPU executes. You are proving the cake is safe to eat, not just that the recipe looks good on paper.</v>

61
00:07:27.797 --> 00:07:42.697
<v Maya>Verify the final product. I love that. But the appendix of this release goes into incredible detail about what they actually proved, and they break it down into three specific theorems to define what no crash actually means. Walk us through those because it's definitely not as simple as it</v>

62
00:07:42.717 --> 00:07:42.978
<v Maya>sounds.</v>

63
00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:50.623
<v Daniel>No, it's pretty involved. So the first theorem is the most straightforward part of the puzzle. They prove that the binary never encounters a fatal fault.</v>

64
00:07:50.943 --> 00:07:51.503
<v Maya>Like what?</v>

65
00:07:51.743 --> 00:08:06.471
<v Daniel>Like trying to access memory that doesn't exist or a sudden abort or any behavior that isn't modeled by the system. But we have to remember, they are verifying fuzz tests, and fuzz tests are almost always compiled with a specialized tool called address sanitizer,</v>

66
00:08:06.711 --> 00:08:07.811
<v Daniel>or ASAN for short.</v>

67
00:08:08.092 --> 00:08:08.812
<v Maya>Okay, ASAN.</v>

68
00:08:09.219 --> 00:08:17.443
<v Daniel>Yeah, ASAN acts kind of like training wheels. It adds a bunch of extra hidden checks into the code to catch subtle memory errors that might normally slip by.</v>

69
00:08:17.843 --> 00:08:25.166
<v Maya>Right, but normal users out in the real world don't run their apps with address sanitizer turned on because all those extra checks slow everything down, right?</v>

70
00:08:25.446 --> 00:08:34.890
<v Daniel>That leads directly to the second theorem. Finding out that the training wheels binary is safe is a great start, but we need to know the normal binary without the training wheels is also safe.</v>

71
00:08:35.473 --> 00:08:35.773
<v Maya>OK.</v>

72
00:08:36.293 --> 00:08:50.738
<v Daniel>So Claude had to mathematically prove that the ASAN instrumented build behaves identically to the normal build, meaning for every single possible input, both versions produce the exact same sequence of memory reads, memory writes, exit codes,</v>

73
00:08:51.278 --> 00:08:53.679
<v Daniel>and leave the computer's file system in the exact same state.</v>

74
00:08:54.239 --> 00:09:03.002
<v Maya>Oh, wow. So if their behaviors are mathematically identical and we know the ASAN version doesn't crash, then logic dictates the normal one doesn't crash either.</v>

75
00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.944
<v Daniel>You would think so. But there was a massive logical loophole there.</v>

76
00:09:07.304 --> 00:09:07.805
<v Maya>Wait, really?</v>

77
00:09:08.185 --> 00:09:23.042
<v Daniel>Yeah, which is why they needed the third theorem. So theorem two says the normal build and the ASAN build produce the exact same trace of events. But what if the ASAN build encounters an error, and instead of crashing, it just gets stuck in a silent, infinite loop?</v>

78
00:09:23.388 --> 00:09:36.345
<v Maya>Oh, I see. Let me try an analogy. It's like two cars driving down parallel roads being tracked by GPS. If one car goes into a tunnel and just drives in circles forever, its GPS trace suddenly just stops transmitting.</v>

79
00:09:36.485 --> 00:09:49.337
<v Daniel>Yes, perfect analogy. The trace for the looping car ends. Now, what if the normal car doesn't loop but actually suffers a catastrophic crash right at that tunnel entrance? Its GPS trace also stops transmitting.</v>

80
00:09:49.577 --> 00:09:49.957
<v Maya>Oh, man.</v>

81
00:09:50.097 --> 00:10:01.824
<v Daniel>Yeah. To a mathematical observer, just comparing the trace logs, both traces ended at the exact same moment. They look equivalent, even though one just got stuck looping and the other one exploded.</v>

82
00:10:01.984 --> 00:10:02.284
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

83
00:10:02.464 --> 00:10:02.644
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

84
00:10:02.924 --> 00:10:09.588
<v Maya>So theorem two might say, everything is fine, the traces match, completely missing the fact that the normal binary actually crashed.</v>

85
00:10:09.967 --> 00:10:22.517
<v Daniel>That is exactly the blind spot. So to mathematically close that loophole, the third theorem independently proves that the normal uninstrumented binary does not crash, preventing that exact false equivalent scenario.</v>

86
00:10:23.197 --> 00:10:35.807
<v Maya>It's like plugging every conceivable logical hole in a dam. But even with all this rigorous math, we still have to trust something, right? In a system this complex, what is the trusted base? Like, what are we assuming is true?</v>

87
00:10:36.278 --> 00:10:51.030
<v Daniel>The trusted base is the foundation. The things you simply have to assume are true for the rest of the proof to hold water. In this specific setup, humans still have to trust the formal mathematical models of how RISC-V chips and Linux operating systems work.</v>

88
00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:51.850
<v Maya>OK, that makes sense.</v>

89
00:10:52.191 --> 00:11:05.441
<v Daniel>You also have to trust that the theorem statements themselves were written correctly. And you have to trust the Rock Proof Checker, which is the software that actually grades Claude's math. But the crucial takeaway here is that you do not have to trust Claude.</v>

90
00:11:05.922 --> 00:11:11.894
<v Maya>Because Claude is just the student submitting the test and Rock acts as the impartial, unhackable judge.</v>

91
00:11:12.175 --> 00:11:15.602
<v Daniel>That's the dynamic. Rock evaluates the logic independently.</v>

92
00:11:15.892 --> 00:11:26.875
<v Maya>But operating at the binary level means Claude is analyzing tens of thousands of machine instructions. To make this work, the way the AI handled the math is just entirely alien to how humans work.</v>

93
00:11:27.076 --> 00:11:27.536
<v Daniel>It really is.</v>

94
00:11:28.076 --> 00:11:40.520
<v Maya>I want to share the scale of this because the numbers are absolutely mind-boggling. So out of roughly a billion lines of rock code written by the AI agents during this project, only about 180 million lines actually pass the checker.</v>

95
00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:44.361
<v Daniel>The sheer volume of generated code is just staggering.</v>

96
00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:57.794
<v Maya>It really is. Listen to this specific example from the appendix. For a tiny little binary program of just 18,000 instructions, a typical proof generated by Claude was 600,000 lines long.</v>

97
00:11:57.914 --> 00:11:58.314
<v Daniel>Incredible.</v>

98
00:11:58.454 --> 00:12:11.458
<v Maya>There's this widely used tool called cJSON, right? Its parser entry point is only 391 lines of code. But Claude generated a 630,000 line proof just to verify those 391 lines.</v>

99
00:12:11.578 --> 00:12:12.698
<v Daniel>Yeah, the inflation is wild.</v>

100
00:12:13.164 --> 00:12:19.428
<v Maya>And just running the proof checker program on one of these proofs takes anywhere from 30 minutes to several hours of raw CPU time.</v>

101
00:12:19.629 --> 00:12:34.539
<v Daniel>The appendix offers a really fascinating view on how this code was actually written by the AI. The agents wrote incredibly verbose repetitive proofs. They would explicitly spell out the exact contents of CPU</v>

102
00:12:34.599 --> 00:12:40.283
<v Daniel>registers over and over again or manually write out integer conversions line by single line.</v>

103
00:12:41.103 --> 00:12:42.064
<v Maya>No shortcuts at all.</v>

104
00:12:42.435 --> 00:12:57.001
<v Daniel>Zero human-like abstractions. A human mathematician or a proof engineer would create shortcuts, right? They'd define a broad concept like a memory allocator and just reference it when needed. Claude didn't do that. It just brute-forced the logic from scratch every single time.</v>

105
00:12:57.281 --> 00:13:10.727
<v Maya>Okay, here's where I have to push back a bit. If these proofs are 600,000 lines long and completely unreadable to human engineers, isn't that a massive problem? What good is a mathematical proof if literally nobody on Earth can read it or comprehend it?</v>

106
00:13:10.955 --> 00:13:14.498
<v Daniel>Well, the authors argue in the release that it actually isn't a problem at all.</v>

107
00:13:14.739 --> 00:13:15.479
<v Maya>Really? How so?</v>

108
00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:30.873
<v Daniel>It goes back to that trusted base we mentioned, the rock checker. The checker is an outside ground truth. The language model cannot argue with the checker, and it cannot hallucinate a passing grade. If those 600,000 lines of</v>

109
00:13:31.194 --> 00:13:35.918
<v Daniel>spaghetti math pass the strict logic of the checker, the code is mathematically safe.</v>

110
00:13:36.287 --> 00:13:37.829
<v Maya>Regardless of how ugly the proof looks.</v>

111
00:13:38.069 --> 00:13:50.943
<v Daniel>Exactly. The readability of the proof simply doesn't change its validity. Plus, while the proofs themselves were huge and ugly, Claude proved highly capable of inventing brilliant, concise specifications.</v>

112
00:13:51.846 --> 00:13:55.707
<v Maya>The specifications being the high-level rules the program is supposed to follow.</v>

113
00:13:55.967 --> 00:14:10.571
<v Daniel>Right. The median specification that Claude wrote was only about 110 lines long for programs that averaged 86 kilobytes of code. So the AI clearly understands the high-level intent of the software perfectly. It just takes a very</v>

114
00:14:10.791 --> 00:14:14.992
<v Daniel>long, very alien, highly granular path to prove it.</v>

115
00:14:15.715 --> 00:14:28.019
<v Maya>But trusting a machine to verify a machine isn't perfectly foolproof. I want to bring up a tension that jumped out at me in footnote six of the release because the researchers ran headfirst into a classic AI alignment problem.</v>

116
00:14:28.539 --> 00:14:29.600
<v Daniel>Oh, the patching issue?</v>

117
00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:38.503
<v Maya>Yes. During this process, Claude wasn't just allowed to read the code. It was allowed to actually patch the code it was trying to prove in order to fix the bugs it found along the way.</v>

118
00:14:38.703 --> 00:14:41.564
<v Daniel>Which introduces a massive philosophical and technical risk.</v>

119
00:14:41.748 --> 00:14:51.578
<v Maya>Right. Because if the A.I.' 's ultimate goal is just make sure this code never crashes and it has the power to edit the code, couldn't it just rewrite the program to do absolutely nothing?</v>

120
00:14:51.798 --> 00:14:52.759
<v Daniel>Yes, it absolutely could.</v>

121
00:14:52.879 --> 00:15:00.046
<v Maya>It's like a mechanic fixing your check engine light by just unscrewing the bulb behind the dashboard. Right. Like, look, the car doesn't report any errors. Job done.</v>

122
00:15:00.567 --> 00:15:15.448
<v Daniel>That is the textbook definition of reward hacking. An AI system will always find the path of least resistance to satisfy the prompt it was given. If the strict goal is don't crash, deleting all the complicated logic that might crash is the easiest</v>

123
00:15:15.488 --> 00:15:16.349
<v Daniel>way to achieve that goal.</v>

124
00:15:16.909 --> 00:15:17.709
<v Maya>So how did they stop it?</v>

125
00:15:18.049 --> 00:15:29.075
<v Daniel>Well, the researchers anticipated this. To combat it, they had to actively penalize heavy patches. And human engineers had to manually audit the changes Claude made to ensure it wasn't just gutting the software.</v>

126
00:15:29.275 --> 00:15:29.595
<v Maya>Got it.</v>

127
00:15:30.319 --> 00:15:44.611
<v Daniel>This really highlights the heart of this entire deep dive. A proof checker is an absolute ground truth, but a proof is only as good as its underlying specification. If the specification secretly allows the program to do nothing,</v>

128
00:15:45.312 --> 00:15:49.375
<v Daniel>the proof is mathematically sound, but entirely meaningless in the real world.</v>

129
00:15:49.779 --> 00:15:58.866
<v Maya>Which brings us to the explicit limitations the authors list in the paper. They are very clear on this point, stating, formally verified does not mean bug-free.</v>

130
00:15:59.086 --> 00:16:06.792
<v Daniel>It does not. For one, these proofs assume single-threaded, static RISC-V binaries linked against a specific C library called Muzzle.</v>

131
00:16:07.232 --> 00:16:12.737
<v Maya>Let's translate that for anyone who isn't a systems engineer. What does linking against Muzzle actually mean?</v>

132
00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:27.630
<v Daniel>So whenever a developer writes a program, they rely on a standard toolkit of pre-written code for basic tasks, like opening a file on the hard drive or printing text to a screen. Muzzle is a very stripped-down, bare-bones version of that toolkit,</v>

133
00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.292
<v Daniel>typically used in tiny, simple embedded devices.</v>

134
00:16:31.773 --> 00:16:32.073
<v Maya>Oh, okay.</v>

135
00:16:32.273 --> 00:16:43.796
<v Daniel>But most of the real-world software you and I interact with, like servers, laptops, smartphones, runs on heavier architectures like x86 or ARM and relies on a much more complex toolkit called glibc.</v>

136
00:16:44.396 --> 00:16:51.358
<v Maya>So they proved this works in a highly controlled, somewhat simplified environment, not the messy real world of standard servers.</v>

137
00:16:51.738 --> 00:17:06.285
<v Daniel>Correct. The proof only guarantees safety in the exact environment it modeled. Furthermore, while address sanitizer is a great tool, it structurally cannot catch every single type of memory bug. And finally, they didn't prove every single entry point in</v>

138
00:17:06.325 --> 00:17:09.847
<v Daniel>these 123 libraries. They only proved a subset of them.</v>

139
00:17:10.247 --> 00:17:21.212
<v Maya>But despite all those limitations, this incredibly rigorous process forced Claude to uncover some truly bizarre edge case bugs, like bugs that traditional fuzzing is just structurally blind to.</v>

140
00:17:21.522 --> 00:17:26.846
<v Daniel>Because as we discussed, a fuzzer would have to literally win the lottery to hit these exact parameters.</v>

141
00:17:27.086 --> 00:17:42.033
<v Maya>Precisely. For example, Claude found a counter underflow in a decompressor program. To trigger this bug, you needed a highly specific 20 megabyte input file that happened to create a very rare memory layout. Wow. Or</v>

142
00:17:42.073 --> 00:17:54.977
<v Maya>consider this one. An image decoder had an out-of-bounds read error that only triggered if you fed it a 64 megabyte input file and the program was actively using over two gigabytes of system memory at that exact moment.</v>

143
00:17:55.517 --> 00:17:59.858
<v Daniel>Fuzzers just don't randomly generate those specific extreme scenarios.</v>

144
00:18:00.078 --> 00:18:03.979
<v Maya>Exactly. Claude also rediscovered an old CVE in LibExpat.</v>

145
00:18:04.847 --> 00:18:19.270
<v Daniel>And for context, CVE is an official, globally recognized record of a specific security flaw. And LibExpat is a foundational tool used by millions of applications to read XML files. Finding flaws there is a big deal.</v>

146
00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:29.552
<v Maya>It also found a file loading restriction that was quietly failing to enforce itself, meaning it wasn't crashing the program at all. It was just a logic bug letting things through that shouldn't get through.</v>

147
00:18:30.152 --> 00:18:30.512
<v Daniel>Sneaky.</v>

148
00:18:30.989 --> 00:18:45.353
<v Maya>Very. And the release includes some fascinating side quests that really expand the scope of this approach. It wasn't just limited to RISC-V binaries. They actually took the Linux kernel's Zlib compression algorithm and formally proved it against a Claude Ritten</v>

